7SDLC Models
__________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________

3 MODELS are popular among the SDLC.

1) Waterfall Model – Linear, sequential approach.
2) Spiral Model – Iterative, risk-driven approach.
3) V-Model – Verification ↔ Validation mapping.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1) Waterfall Model

The Waterfall Model is a linear, sequential approach to software development. 
Each phase must be completed before moving to the next.
Resembling a "waterfall" flow.

Phases
1 Requirement Analysis
2 System Design
3 Implementation
4 Testing
5 Deployment
6 Maintenance

✅ Advantages
High product quality due to structured phases.Ensure thorough documentation and clarity.
Reduced bugs since requirement changes are not allowed./ Since requirements are fixed, fewer chances of scope creep.
Lower initial investment as Testers are hired later in the cycle.
Best for small projects with fixed requirements. Works well when requirements are stable and clearly defined.

❌ Disadvantages
No Requirement Changes – Inflexible once the process starts.
Defect Propagation – Errors in requirements carry forward into later stages.
High Rework Cost – Fixing defects late in the cycle is expensive.
Late Testing – Testing begins only after coding, delaying defect detection.
____________________________________________________________________________________________________

2) 🔄 Spiral Model

The Spiral Model is an iterative model that overcomes drawbacks of the Waterfall model.
Used when there is dependency on modules.
Software is released in multiple versions → also called Version Control Model.

The Spiral Model is often depicted as a spiral with four quadrants:
Planning
Risk Analysis
Engineering & Execution
Evaluation

✅ Advantages
Testing in every cycle before moving to the next.
Customer feedback after each module.
Requirement changes allowed after every cycle.

❌ Disadvantages
No mid-cycle changes allowed.
Each cycle resembles the Waterfall Model.
No testing in requirement & design phases.
