3) V-Model in Software Development

The V-Model is a structured approach to the Software Development Life Cycle. 
Unlike the linear Waterfall Model, the V-Model integrates verification and validation activities alongside each development phase.
This ensures that errors are caught early and requirements are continuously checked against deliverables. 

🏗️ Phases of the V-Model

🔹 Verification (Left Side of V)
Ensures we are building the right product.
Focuses on documentation, reviews, walkthroughs, inspections.
Business Requirement Analysis → Defines customer needs, acceptance test planning.
System Design → High-level design of the system.
Architectural Design → High-Level Design (HLD), module interactions defined.
Module Design → Low-Level Design (LLD), detailed specifications for each module.

🔹 Validation (Right Side of V)
Ensures we are building the product right. 
Focuses on actual software testing (unit, integration, system, UAT).
Unit Testing → Validates module design.
Integration Testing → Validates architectural design.
System Testing → Validates system design.
Acceptance Testing → Validates business requirements. 

✅ Advantages
Early defect detection due to parallel testing.
High reliability for safety-critical projects (medical, automotive, aerospace).
Clear structure with strong documentation.

❌ Disadvantages
Rigid process → Requirement changes are costly.
High documentation overhead.
Not suitable for small or rapidly changing projects.
